<handout>
<title>Multiplying and Dividing Rational Expressions</title>
<page>
    <objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this section we will see how to:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Multiply and simplify rational expressions
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Divide and simplify rational expressions.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>
<p>
    To multiply rational expressions, we simply multiply the numerators together and the denominators together. But if we want a simplified result. We first want to cancel
    out any common factors first and then multiply the result which guarantees a simplified result.
</p>
<example workspace="3.0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Multiply and simplify <m>\frac{x+8}{x+9}\cdot \frac{2x+18}{3x+24}</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>, <m>x \ne -8, -9</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            First factor everything to get <m>\frac{(x+8)(2)(x+9)}{(x+9)(3)(x+8)}</m>. Cancel out common factors to get \frac{2}{3} and also state that <m>x \ne -8, -9</m>
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
</page>
<page>
<example workspace="2.0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Multiply and simplify <m>\frac{5x}{x^2+5x+6}\cdot \frac{x^2-4}{10x}</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>\frac{x-2}{2x+6}</m>, <m>x\ne -2, 0</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Start by factoring <m>\frac{(5x)(x+2)(x-2)}{(x+2)(x+3)(2)(5x)}</m>. Cancel out the common factors: <m>\frac{x-2}{2(x+3)}</m>. Multiply the factors in the 
            denominator together to reach the final answer <m>\frac{x-2}{2x+6}</m> and state that <m>x\ne -2, 0</m>
        </p>
        </solution>
</example>
<example workspace="2.0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Multiply and simplify <m>\frac{x^2+8x+15}{x+5}\cdot \frac{2}{x+3}</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>2</m>, <m>x \ne -3, -5</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Start by factoring <m>\frac{(x+3)(x+5)(2)}{(x+5)(x+3)}</m>. Cancel out the common factors and we are left with only 2 in the numerator and state that <m>x\ne -2, 0</m>.
        </p>
        </solution>
</example>
<example workspace="2.0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Multiply and simplify <m>\frac{x-7}{4x}\cdot \frac{x^2+3x}{x^2-10x+21}</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>\frac{x+3}{4x-12}</m>, <m>x \ne 0, 7</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Start by factoring <m>\frac{(x-7)(x)(x+3)}{(4)(x)(x-7)(x-3)}</m>. Cancel out the common factors and multiply what is left over to get <m>\frac{x+3}{4x-12}</m>
            and state that <m>x\ne 0, 7</m>.
        </p>
        </solution>
</example>
</page>
<page>
    <p>
        There is one extra step when dividing rationals. Recall that when you divide two fractions, it is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction and the reciprocal
        of the second fraction. So to guarantee a simplified result, we swap the numerator and denominator, factor and cancel and then multiply whatever is left over.
    </p>
    <example workspace="2.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
              Divide and simplify <m>\frac{x}{7}\div\frac{x+4}{35}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{5x}{x+4}</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Take the reciprocal of the second fraction to get <m>\frac{x}{7}\cdot\frac{35}{x+4}</m>. Factor and cancel out the 7's and we are left with <m>\frac{5x}{x+4}</m>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
    <example workspace="2.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
              Divide and simplify <m>\frac{x^2-9}{15}\div\frac{x^3-27}{5x^2+15x+45}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{x+3}{3}</m>, <m>x\ne 3</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Take the reciprocal of the second fraction to get <m>\frac{x^2-9}{15}\cdot\frac{5x^2+15x+45}{x^3-27}</m>. Factor to get <m>\frac{(x+3)(x-3)(5)(x^2+3x+9)}{(15)(x-3)(x^2+3x+9)}</m>. 
                Cancel out the common factors while stating <m>x\ne 3</m> and we are left with <m>\frac{x+3}{3}</m>. Note that <m>x^2+3x+9</m> is never equal to zero.
                </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
    </page>
    <page>
    <example workspace="3.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
              Divide and simplify <m>\frac{x^3+8}{3x^2-6x+12}\div\frac{x^2-4}{6}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{2}{x-2}</m>, <m>x\ne -2</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Take the reciprocal of the second fraction to get <m>\frac{x^3+8}{3x^2-6x+12}\cdot\frac{6}{x^2-4}</m>. Factor to get <m>\frac{(x+2)(x^2-2x+4)(6)}{(3)(x^2-2x+4)(x+2)(x-2)}</m>. 
                Cancel out the common factors while stating <m>x\ne -2</m> and we are left with <m>\frac{2}{x-2}</m>. Note that <m>x^2-2x+4</m> is never equal to zero.
                </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
    <example workspace="3.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
              Divide and simplify <m>\frac{x^3+8}{3x^2-6x+12}\div\frac{x^2-4}{6}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{2}{x-2}</m>, <m>x\ne -2</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Take the reciprocal of the second fraction to get <m>\frac{x^3+8}{3x^2-6x+12}\cdot\frac{6}{x^2-4}</m>. Factor to get <m>\frac{(x+2)(x^2-2x+4)(6)}{(3)(x^2-2x+4)(x+2)(x-2)}</m>. 
                Cancel out the common factors while stating <m>x\ne -2</m> and we are left with <m>\frac{2}{x-2}</m>. Note that <m>x^2-2x+4</m> is never equal to zero.
                </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
</page>
<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Try it Now!</title>
        <p>
          1. Multiply and simplify. Make sure to state where the denominator is zero when you cancel out factors.
        </p>
        <p workspace="2.25in">
         (a)   <m>\frac{5x^2}{x^2-5x-36}\cdot \frac{x^2-16}{10x}</m>
        </p>
        <p workspace="2.25in">
         (b)   <m>\frac{x^2+3x}{x^2-3x-4}\cdot \frac{x-4}{x^2}</m>
        </p>
        <p workspace="2.25in">
         (c)   <m>\frac{2x^2+x-3}{x^2-16}\cdot \frac{x^2-8x+16}{2x^2-9x-18}</m>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>
    </page>
    <page>
        <p>
          2.  Divide and simplify. Make sure to state where the denominator is zero when you cancel out factors.
        </p>
        <p workspace="2.25in">
          (a) <m>\frac{x^2-81}{x^2-6x-27}\div \frac{4x}{x+3}</m>
        </p>
        <p workspace="2.25in">
          (b) <m>\frac{x-9}{3}\div \frac{9-x}{18}</m>
        </p>
        <p workspace="2.25in">
         (c) <m>\frac{3x^2}{x^2-16}\div \frac{x^3+4x^2+16x}{x^3-64}</m>
        </p>
        </page>
</handout>